    "thm-frobenius-projection-onto-psd-cone-is-spectral-clipping": {
      "risk": "medium",
      "kind": "theorem",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real inner product space and let $T:V\\to V$ be\nself-adjoint. Then $V$ has an orthonormal basis consisting of eigenvectors of\n$T$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-non-negative-operator-characterisations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real or complex inner product space and let\n$T:V\\to V$ be linear. The following are equivalent:\n\n1. $T$ is non-negative.\n2. $T$ is self-adjoint and every eigenvalue of $T$ is a nonnegative real number.\n3. Some orthonormal basis makes the matrix of $T$ diagonal with nonnegative real diagonal entries.\n4. There exists a linear map $S:V\\to V$ such that $T=S^*S$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "prop-operator-positivity-agrees-with-form-positivity-over-the-reals",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real inner product space. The assignment\n\n$$T\\longmapsto B_T,\\qquad B_T(u,v):=\\langle Tu,v\\rangle,$$\n\nrestricts to a bijection between self-adjoint endomorphisms of $V$ and\nsymmetric bilinear forms on $V$. Under this bijection, non-negative operators\ncorrespond exactly to positive semidefinite forms, and positive operators\ncorrespond exactly to positive definite forms.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-spectral-and-frobenius-norms-are-unitarily-invariant-with-singular-value-formulas-and-rank-comparison",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathbb F$ be $\\mathbb R$ or $\\mathbb C$, let $A\\in M_{m\\times n}(\\mathbb F)$, and\nlet $\\sigma_1\\ge\\sigma_2\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\sigma_r>0$ be the positive singular values of\n$A$, where $r=\\operatorname{rank}A$; write $\\sigma_1=0$ when $r=0$.\n\n1. **Spectral formula.** $\\lVert A\\rVert_2=\\sigma_1$, where $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ is\n   the operator norm of The operator norm is zero on the zero domain and otherwise is max_{||v||=1} ||Tv||.\n2. **Frobenius formula.** $\\lVert A\\rVert_F=\\bigl(\\sigma_1^{2}+\\cdots+\\sigma_r^{2}\\bigr)^{1/2}$.\n3. **Unitary invariance.** For every unitary $U\\in M_m(\\mathbb F)$ and\n   $V\\in M_n(\\mathbb F)$ (over $\\mathbb F=\\mathbb R$: orthogonal matrices),\n   $$\\lVert UAV\\rVert_2=\\lVert A\\rVert_2,\\qquad \\lVert UAV\\rVert_F=\\lVert A\\rVert_F.$$\n4. **Sharp rank comparison.**\n   $$\\lVert A\\rVert_2\\;\\le\\;\\lVert A\\rVert_F\\;\\le\\;\\sqrt{r}\\,\\lVert A\\rVert_2.$$\n   The lower inequality is an equality exactly when $r\\le 1$; the upper\n   inequality is an equality exactly when $\\sigma_1=\\cdots=\\sigma_r$, that is,\n   when all nonzero singular values coincide. In particular\n   $\\lVert A\\rVert_F\\le\\sqrt{\\min(m,n)}\\,\\lVert A\\rVert_2$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-frobenius-matrix-norm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathbb F$ be $\\mathbb R$ or $\\mathbb C$, let $m,n\\in\\mathbb{N}$ and let\n$A=(a_{ij})\\in M_{m\\times n}(\\mathbb F)$. The **Frobenius norm** of $A$ is\n\n$$\\lVert A\\rVert_F\\;:=\\;\\Bigl(\\,\\sum_{i<m}\\sum_{j<n}|a_{ij}|^{2}\\Bigr)^{1/2},$$\n\nwhere $|\\cdot|$ is the absolute value on $\\mathbb R$\n(Absolute value in an ordered field) for $\\mathbb F=\\mathbb R$ and the modulus on $\\mathbb C$\n(Real and imaginary parts, complex conjugation, and modulus) for\n$\\mathbb F=\\mathbb C$, and the outer power is the nonnegative real square root.\n\n**The displayed quantity is a norm.** Regard $A$ as the element of the\ncoordinate space $\\mathbb F^{mn}$ whose $j+in$-th entry is $a_{ij}$. The\nstandard coordinate inner product of The standard formulas $\\langle x,y\\rangle=\\sum_{k<n}x_k y_k$ on $\\mathbb R^n$ and $\\sum_{k<n}x_k\\overline{y_k}$ on $\\mathbb C^n$ are inner products\nsatisfies $\\langle A,A\\rangle=\\sum_{i,j}a_{ij}\\overline{a_{ij}}$, which equals\n$\\sum_{i,j}|a_{ij}|^{2}$ because $z\\overline z=|z|^{2}$\n(Conjugation is an involutive real-field automorphism, $z\\overline z=|z|^2$, and modulus is definite, multiplicative, and subadditive). Thus $\\lVert A\\rVert_F$ is\nexactly the inner-product norm of The norm $\\lVert v\\rVert=\\sqrt{\\langle v,v\\rangle}$ induced by a real or complex inner product evaluated at $A$,\nand the three norm axioms hold for $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_F$ because they hold for\nevery inner-product norm. For real matrices the same sum is\n$\\sum_{i,j}a_{ij}^{2}$, since $|x|^{2}=x^{2}$ for real $x$ by the two cases of\nAbsolute value in an ordered field.\n\nThe Frobenius norm is the Euclidean norm of the full entry list, not the\nlargest factor by which the matrix stretches a single vector. It can coincide\nwith an induced $2$-norm in one-row or one-column cases, but in general it is a\ndifferent matrix norm from the induced operator norms used on this page. Its\nrelation to the spectral norm, its unitary invariance and its singular-value\nformula are\nSpectral and Frobenius norms are unitarily invariant, are given by singular values, and satisfy the sharp rank comparison.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F1], choose an orthogonal eigendecomposition of $A$ and define $A_+$ as in the Statement. It is symmetric and has eigenvalues $\\lambda_i^+\\ge0$. By [F2] its associated operator is nonnegative, and by [F3] its quadratic form is positive semidefinite; hence $A_+\\in\\mathbb S_+^d$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Let $Z\\in\\mathbb S_+^d$ and set $W=U^TZU$. For each coordinate vector $e_i$, $W_{ii}=e_i^TWe_i=(Ue_i)^TZ(Ue_i)\\ge0$; also $W$ is symmetric. Orthogonal invariance [F4] gives $\\|A-Z\\|_F^2=\\|\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_d)-W\\|_F^2$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Expanding the entry sum [F5], the last square is $\\sum_i(\\lambda_i-W_{ii})^2+\\sum_{i\\ne j}W_{ij}^2$. For every $w\\ge0$, $(\\lambda_i-w)^2\\ge(\\lambda_i-\\lambda_i^+)^2$: if $\\lambda_i\\ge0$ the right side is zero and equality is attained only at $w=\\lambda_i$, while if $\\lambda_i<0$ then $w-\\lambda_i\\ge-\\lambda_i$ and equality is attained only at $w=0$. Thus every feasible $Z$ has squared distance at least $\\sum_i(\\lambda_i-\\lambda_i^+)^2$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For $Z=A_+$ the conjugated matrix $W$ is $\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1^+,\\ldots,\\lambda_d^+)$, so all off-diagonal terms vanish and every diagonal term attains its lower bound from step 2.1. Hence $A_+$ is a minimizer.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Equality in the lower bound requires every off-diagonal square in step 2.1 to vanish and every diagonal term to attain its unique scalar minimum. Therefore $W=\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1^+,\\ldots,\\lambda_d^+)$ and $Z=U W U^T=A_+$. The minimizer is unique; since the argument applies to any orthogonal eigendecomposition, every allowed decomposition gives this same nearest matrix.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "reason": "The cone is nonempty because the zero matrix is positive semidefinite. At d=1 the off-diagonal square sum in step 2.1 is empty and equals zero; the remaining scalar minimization on w at least zero still yields the unique clipped eigenvalue in steps 2.1 and 4.1.",
          "evidence": "The cone is nonempty because the zero matrix is positive semidefinite. At d=1 the off-diagonal square sum in step 2.1 is empty and equals zero; the remaining scalar minimization on w at least zero still yields the unique clipped eigenvalue in steps 2.1 and 4.1.",
