    "lem-svec-transports-frobenius-subgradients": {
      "risk": "medium",
      "kind": "lemma",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-symmetric-matrix-frobenius-isometry",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$, let $\\mathbb S^d$ be the real vector space of symmetric $d\\times d$ matrices, and set $m=d(d+1)/2$. Use one-based entry labels $A_{ij}:=A(i-1,j-1)$ for $1\\le i,j\\le d$, and likewise for every matrix below. Equip $\\mathbb S^d$ with the Frobenius inner product $\\langle A,B\\rangle_F:=\\sum_{i=1}^{d}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}A_{ij}B_{ij}$ and its induced norm. Define $\\operatorname{svec}:\\mathbb S^d\\to\\mathbb R^m$ by listing $A_{11},\\ldots,A_{dd}$ first, followed by $\\sqrt2 A_{ij}$ for $1\\le i<j\\le d$ in lexicographic order. Then $\\operatorname{svec}$ is a linear bijection and preserves inner products and norms; in particular, $\\|\\operatorname{svec}(A)-\\operatorname{svec}(B)\\|_2=\\|A-B\\|_F$ for all $A,B\\in\\mathbb S^d$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-convex-and-strictly-convex-functions-on-euclidean-sets",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $C\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be convex (A convex subset of $\\mathbb{R}^m$ contains every line segment between two of its points). The function $f:C\\to\\mathbb R$ is convex when $f((1-t)x+ty)\\le(1-t)f(x)+tf(y)$ for all $x,y\\in C$ and $t\\in[0,1]$. The parameter interval is the closed interval of Intervals of $\\mathbb{R}$: the nine order-convex forms, nondegeneracy, and length.\n\nIt is strictly convex when the inequality is strict for distinct $x,y$ and $0<t<1$. No strict inequality is required at $t=0$ or $t=1$, where the two sides coincide. The empty set and a singleton support convex functions, and strict convexity on either is vacuous.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A subset $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ is **convex** when, for all $x,y\\in U$ and $t\\in[0,1]$ (Intervals of $\\mathbb{R}$: the nine order-convex forms, nondegeneracy, and length), the point $(1-t)x+ty$ lies in $U$. Thus the full line segment from $x$ to $y$ remains in $U$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-metric-topology",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space (Metric space: $d(x,y) = 0$ iff $x = y$, symmetry, and the triangle inequality; pseudometric and ultrametric).\n\nA subset $U \\subseteq X$ is **open in $(X,d)$** if for every $x \\in U$ there is a\nreal $r > 0$ with $B(x,r) \\subseteq U$ (Open ball, closed ball and sphere in a metric space). A subset\n$F \\subseteq X$ is **closed in $(X,d)$** if its complement $X \\setminus F$ is\nopen.\n\nThe collection\n\n$$\\mathcal{T}_d := \\{\\, U \\subseteq X : U \\text{ is open in } (X,d) \\,\\}$$\n\nof all open subsets is the **metric topology** of $d$ on $X$. A subset of $X$\nthat is both open and closed is called **clopen**.\n\n**Two sets are open for trivial reasons.** $\\emptyset$ is open, because the\ndefining condition quantifies over no points; and $X$ is open, because\n$B(x,r) \\subseteq X$ for every $x$ and every $r > 0$. Consequently $X$ and\n$\\emptyset$ are also closed, and both are clopen.\n\n**A neighbourhood of a point $x$** is any set containing an open set that\ncontains $x$; an **open neighbourhood** is an open set containing $x$. In a\nmetric space, a set is a neighbourhood of $x$ exactly when it contains some\nball $B(x,r)$ with $r>0$. Thus $U$ is open exactly when it is a neighbourhood of\neach of its points, and metric balls suffice to test this condition.\n\n**The metric, not the set, determines $\\mathcal{T}_d$.** Two metrics on the same\nset may have different metric topologies, and two different metrics may have the\nsame one; the systematic comparison is Topologically, uniformly and Lipschitz equivalent metrics on a set.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-metric-ball",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space (Metric space: $d(x,y) = 0$ iff $x = y$, symmetry, and the triangle inequality; pseudometric and ultrametric), let $x \\in X$ and let\n$r \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $r > 0$ (Order on the reals). Define\n\n$$B(x,r) := \\{\\, y \\in X : d(x,y) < r \\,\\}, \\qquad \\bar B(x,r) := \\{\\, y \\in X : d(x,y) \\le r \\,\\}, \\qquad S(x,r) := \\{\\, y \\in X : d(x,y) = r \\,\\}.$$\n\n$B(x,r)$ is the **open ball**, $\\bar B(x,r)$ the **closed ball** and $S(x,r)$ the\n**sphere** of **centre** $x$ and **radius** $r$. The radius is always a strictly\npositive real; a ball of radius $0$ or of negative radius is never written in\nthis library.\n\n**Immediate consequences of the definitions.** For every $x \\in X$ and $r > 0$:\n\n- $x \\in B(x,r)$, because $d(x,x) = 0 < r$ (axiom (M1) of Metric space: $d(x,y) = 0$ iff $x = y$, symmetry, and the triangle inequality; pseudometric and ultrametric);\n  in particular open and closed balls are nonempty.\n- $B(x,r) \\subseteq \\bar B(x,r)$ and $S(x,r) \\subseteq \\bar B(x,r)$, and\n  $\\bar B(x,r)$ is the disjoint union of $B(x,r)$ and $S(x,r)$, by trichotomy of\n  the order of $\\mathbb{R}$ (Complete ordered field (least-upper-bound property), Ordered field):\n  each $y$ satisfies exactly one of $d(x,y) < r$, $d(x,y) = r$, $d(x,y) > r$.\n- If $0 < s \\le r$ then $B(x,s) \\subseteq B(x,r)$ and\n  $\\bar B(x,s) \\subseteq \\bar B(x,r)$, by transitivity of the order.\n- Nonnegativity of the metric (Nonnegativity of a metric is a consequence of the other axioms, not an axiom) is what forces the\n  radius convention, and it forces it for the **open** ball only: if $r \\le 0$\n  then $B(x,r) = \\{y : d(x,y) < r\\}$ is empty, because $d(x,y) \\ge 0 \\ge r$\n  for every $y$. The other two sets behave differently at $r = 0$, and the\n  convention $r > 0$ excludes them for uniformity rather than for emptiness:\n  $\\bar B(x,0) = S(x,0) = \\{x\\}$, since $d(x,y) \\le 0$ together with\n  $d(x,y) \\ge 0$ gives $d(x,y) = 0$ and hence $y = x$ by (M1). For $r < 0$ all\n  three sets are empty.\n\n**A sphere may be empty, and so the three sets are not on a par.** For $r > 0$\nthe open and closed balls always contain $x$, but nothing in the definition\nproduces a point at distance exactly $r$ from $x$. If a metric takes only the\nvalues $0$ and $1$, as the discrete metric on the companion page does, then\n$S(x,2) = \\emptyset$ while $B(x,2) = \\bar B(x,2)$ is the whole space. So\nnonemptiness of a sphere is never available by convention: where it is used, it\nis proved.\n\n**The ambient space is part of the notation.** $B(x,r)$ depends on $(X,d)$ and\nnot on $x$ and $r$ alone. When more than one space or more than one metric is in\nplay we write $B_X(x,r)$, or $B_d(x,r)$, and likewise for $\\bar B$ and $S$. This\nmatters as soon as subspaces appear (Isometry, isometric embedding, and the subspace metric on a subset): a\nball of a subspace is the trace on it of a ball of the ambient space, and the two\nare different sets.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-subgradient-and-subdifferential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $f:C\\to\\mathbb R$ be convex on a convex set $C\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ (Convex and strictly convex functions on Euclidean convex sets), and let $a\\in C$. A vector $v$ is a subgradient of $f$ at $a$ when $f(y)\\ge f(a)+\\langle v,y-a\\rangle$ for every $y$ in the domain.\n\nThe **subdifferential** is the set\n\n$$\\partial f(a):=\\{v\\in\\mathbb R^n:f(y)\\ge f(a)+\\langle v,y-a\\rangle\\text{ for every }y\\in C\\},$$\n\nwith the Euclidean inner product of The Euclidean inner product $\\langle x,y\\rangle = \\sum_{k<n} x_k y_k$ on $\\mathbb{R}^n$. The definition permits $\\partial f(a)$ to be empty or to contain more than one vector.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "If $z_0,z_1\\in C'$, choose $M_0,M_1\\in C$ with $z_i=\\operatorname{svec}(M_i)$. For $t\\in[0,1]$, linearity gives $tz_0+(1-t)z_1=\\operatorname{svec}(tM_0+(1-t)M_1)$, and the matrix on the right is in $C$ by [F2]. Thus $C'$ is convex.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Let $z\\notin C'$ and set $M=\\operatorname{svec}^{-1}(z)\\notin C$. Since $C$ is closed, [F3] gives $r>0$ such that $B_F(M,r)\\subseteq\\mathbb S^d\\setminus C$, where $B_F(M,r)=\\{A\\in\\mathbb S^d:\\|A-M\\|_F<r\\}$. For any $w\\in B_2(z,r)$, isometry [F1] gives $\\operatorname{svec}^{-1}(w)\\in B_F(M,r)$, hence $w\\notin C'$. Therefore $B_2(z,r)\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m\\setminus C'$, so the complement of $C'$ is open and $C'$ is closed.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "For $z_0,z_1\\in C'$ with preimages $M_0,M_1$, linearity yields $\\operatorname{svec}^{-1}(tz_0+(1-t)z_1)=tM_0+(1-t)M_1$. Applying convexity of $F$ from [given] proves $f(tz_0+(1-t)z_1)\\le t f(z_0)+(1-t)f(z_1)$, so $f$ is convex.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Let $z=\\operatorname{svec}(M)$ and take any $w\\in C'$, with preimage $N\\in C$. The assumed subgradient inequality gives $f(w)=F(N)\\ge F(M)+\\langle G,N-M\\rangle_F$. By inner-product preservation [F1], this equals $f(z)+\\langle\\operatorname{svec}(G),w-z\\rangle_2$; hence $\\operatorname{svec}(G)$ is a Euclidean subgradient by [F4].",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F4"
          ]
        }
      ],
