    "thm-lmnn-fixed-target-objective-is-convex": {
      "risk": "medium",
      "kind": "theorem",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-lmnn-fixed-target-objective",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix positive integers $d,n,C$, a finite labeled sample $(x_i,y_i)_{i=1}^{n}$ with $x_i\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and $y_i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,C\\}$, and a weight $\\mu\\in[0,1]$. For each $i$, fix in advance an integer $k_i$ with $0\\le k_i\\le|\\{j\\ne i:y_j=y_i\\}|$ and a fixed target-neighbor set $N_i\\subseteq\\{j\\ne i:y_j=y_i\\}$ with $|N_i|=k_i$. These target sets do not change with the matrix being optimized. Let $\\mathbb S_+^d$ denote the real symmetric matrices $M$ satisfying $v^TMv\\ge0$ for all $v\\in\\mathbb R^d$, and put $q_M(x,x')=(x-x')^TM(x-x')$. For a real number $z$, let $[z]_+=\\max\\{z,0\\}$. For $M\\in\\mathbb S_+^d$, define\n\n$$\\epsilon_{\\rm pull}(M)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\sum_{j\\in N_i}q_M(x_i,x_j),\\qquad \\epsilon_{\\rm push}(M)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\sum_{j\\in N_i}\\sum_{\\substack{1\\le l\\le n\\\\y_l\\ne y_i}}[1+q_M(x_i,x_j)-q_M(x_i,x_l)]_+,$$\n\nand $F_\\mu(M)=(1-\\mu)\\epsilon_{\\rm pull}(M)+\\mu\\epsilon_{\\rm push}(M)$. This is the fixed-target LMNN training surrogate; it is not a test-error or calibrated/generalization-risk quantity. The quantity $q_M$ is a squared distance, while its square root is the Mahalanobis pseudometric.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-trace-of-a-square-matrix",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a square matrix $A=(a_{ij})\\in M_n(F)$, its **trace** is the sum of its\ndiagonal entries,\n\n$$\\operatorname{tr}(A):=\\sum_{i\\in n}a_{ii}.$$\n\nFor $n=0$, this is the empty sum, so the unique empty matrix has trace $0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-convex-and-strictly-convex-functions-on-euclidean-sets",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $C\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be convex (A convex subset of $\\mathbb{R}^m$ contains every line segment between two of its points). The function $f:C\\to\\mathbb R$ is convex when $f((1-t)x+ty)\\le(1-t)f(x)+tf(y)$ for all $x,y\\in C$ and $t\\in[0,1]$. The parameter interval is the closed interval of Intervals of $\\mathbb{R}$: the nine order-convex forms, nondegeneracy, and length.\n\nIt is strictly convex when the inequality is strict for distinct $x,y$ and $0<t<1$. No strict inequality is required at $t=0$ or $t=1$, where the two sides coincide. The empty set and a singleton support convex functions, and strict convexity on either is vacuous.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A subset $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ is **convex** when, for all $x,y\\in U$ and $t\\in[0,1]$ (Intervals of $\\mathbb{R}$: the nine order-convex forms, nondegeneracy, and length), the point $(1-t)x+ty$ lies in $U$. Thus the full line segment from $x$ to $y$ remains in $U$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For each fixed pair $(i,j)$, let $C_{ij}=(x_i-x_j)(x_i-x_j)^T$. By [F1], $q_M(x_i,x_j)=\\operatorname{tr}(MC_{ij})$ is linear in $M$, so every pull term is affine-linear. Each margin expression $h_{ijl}(M)=1+\\operatorname{tr}(M(C_{ij}-C_{il}))$ is also affine-linear.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For any affine-linear $h$ and $0\\le t\\le1$, $h(tM+(1-t)N)=t h(M)+(1-t)h(N)$. The number $t[h(M)]_++(1-t)[h(N)]_+$ is at least $0$ and at least $t h(M)+(1-t)h(N)$, hence it is at least their maximum $[h(tM+(1-t)N)]_+$. Thus each hinge term is convex by [F2].",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
