          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets (Finite, countably infinite, countable, uncountable) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-lmnn-fixed-target-objective-is-convex",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d,n,C$ be positive integers, let $(x_i,y_i)_{i=1}^{n}$ be a finite sample with $x_i\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and $y_i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,C\\}$, let each fixed target set satisfy $N_i\\subseteq\\{j\\ne i:y_j=y_i\\}$, and let $\\mu\\in[0,1]$. For symmetric $M\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$ write $q_M(x,x')=(x-x')^TM(x-x')$ and define\n\n$$F_\\mu(M)=(1-\\mu)\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\sum_{j\\in N_i}q_M(x_i,x_j)+\\mu\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\sum_{j\\in N_i}\\sum_{\\substack{1\\le l\\le n\\\\y_l\\ne y_i}}[1+q_M(x_i,x_j)-q_M(x_i,x_l)]_+,$$\n\nwhere $[z]_+=\\max\\{z,0\\}$. Then $F_\\mu$ is convex on the real vector space of symmetric matrices. The feasible set $\\mathbb S_+^d=\\{M=M^T:v^TMv\\ge0\\text{ for all }v\\in\\mathbb R^d\\}$ is convex, so minimizing $F_\\mu$ over $\\mathbb S_+^d$ is a convex optimization problem.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-positive-semidefinite-cone-is-frobenius-closed",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For an integer $d\\ge1$, let $\\mathbb S^d$ be the real symmetric $d\\times d$ matrices and let $\\mathbb S_+^d=\\{M\\in\\mathbb S^d:v^TMv\\ge0\\text{ for every }v\\in\\mathbb R^d\\}$. With the Frobenius norm $\\|A\\|_F=(\\sum_{i,j}A_{ij}^2)^{1/2}$, the set $\\mathbb S_+^d$ is closed in $\\mathbb S^d$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-symmetric-matrix-frobenius-isometry",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$, let $\\mathbb S^d$ be the real vector space of symmetric $d\\times d$ matrices, and set $m=d(d+1)/2$. Use one-based entry labels $A_{ij}:=A(i-1,j-1)$ for $1\\le i,j\\le d$, and likewise for every matrix below. Equip $\\mathbb S^d$ with the Frobenius inner product $\\langle A,B\\rangle_F:=\\sum_{i=1}^{d}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}A_{ij}B_{ij}$ and its induced norm. Define $\\operatorname{svec}:\\mathbb S^d\\to\\mathbb R^m$ by listing $A_{11},\\ldots,A_{dd}$ first, followed by $\\sqrt2 A_{ij}$ for $1\\le i<j\\le d$ in lexicographic order. Then $\\operatorname{svec}$ is a linear bijection and preserves inner products and norms; in particular, $\\|\\operatorname{svec}(A)-\\operatorname{svec}(B)\\|_2=\\|A-B\\|_F$ for all $A,B\\in\\mathbb S^d$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-svec-transports-frobenius-subgradients",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $C\\subseteq\\mathbb S^d$ be a closed convex set of real symmetric matrices in the Frobenius metric, let $F:C\\to\\mathbb R$ be convex, let $\\operatorname{svec}:\\mathbb S^d\\to\\mathbb R^m$ be the symmetric vectorization isometry, and define $C'=\\operatorname{svec}(C)$ and $f:C'\\to\\mathbb R$ by $f(z)=F(\\operatorname{svec}^{-1}(z))$. Then $C'$ is closed and convex and $f$ is convex. If $G$ is a Frobenius subgradient of $F$ at $M\\in C$, meaning $F(N)\\ge F(M)+\\langle G,N-M\\rangle_F$ for every $N\\in C$, then $\\operatorname{svec}(G)$ is a Euclidean subgradient of $f$ at $\\operatorname{svec}(M)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-euclidean-space-complete",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "1. $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$ with the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]) is a complete metric space\n   ([[def-complete-metric-space]]).\n2. Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on\n   $\\mathbb{R}^n$ ([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ is complete.\n\n**The hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited and is not decoration.**\n[[lem-metrics-on-rn]] defines $\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its three metrics only for\n$n \\ge 1$, because at $n = 0$ the metric $d_\\infty$ would be a maximum over the\nempty index set. Every statement about $\\mathbb{R}^n$ in this library carries the\nhypothesis, and this one does too.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-hilbert-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **real Hilbert space** is a real inner-product space $H$ (Real and complex inner-product spaces and their induced length) whose induced-length metric is complete in the sense of Complete metric space: every Cauchy sequence converges in the space: every Cauchy sequence in $H$ for the norm $\\|v\\|=\\sqrt{\\langle v,v\\rangle}$ (The induced length is a norm) converges to a point of $H$. A **complex Hilbert space** is a complex inner-product space with the same completeness property, so that a Hilbert space is exactly a real or complex inner-product space that is a Banach space for its induced norm (Banach space).\n\n**The completion convention is the Cauchy-sequence one.**\nBanach space defines completeness by convergence of Cauchy sequences, and this page keeps that convention throughout. It is weaker than $\\sigma$-completeness — the assertion that every decreasing sequence of nonempty closed subsets with diameters tending to zero has a nonempty intersection. The two agree in ZFC, but Blackadar, Farah and Karagila note that the closest-point theorem on a $\\sigma$-complete inner-product space is provable in ZF, whereas the Cauchy-complete form used below consumes the Axiom of Countable Choice; nothing here silently imports the stronger notion.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. A natural number is a von Neumann natural, that is a set,\nand $n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ (The natural numbers $\\mathbb{N}$ (von Neumann),\nOn $\\mathbb{N}$ the order is membership: $m < n \\iff m \\in n$), so\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\;=\\; \\{\\, x \\;:\\; x \\text{ is a function } n \\to \\mathbb{R} \\,\\}$$\n\nis the function space of The vector space $F^{X}$ of all functions $X \\to F$ with pointwise operations, and $F^{n}$ as the case $X = n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ at $F = \\mathbb{R}$ and $X = n$,\na vector space over $\\mathbb{R}$ under the pointwise operations\n(Vector space over a field). We write $x_k := x(k)$ for $k < n$, and two elements of\n$\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ are equal exactly when they agree at every $k < n$. This is the\nsame set that $\\mathbb{R}^n$ as the set of functions $n \\to \\mathbb{R}$, and $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ are metrics on it calls $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$.\n\nThe **Euclidean inner product** of $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is the real number\n\n$$\\langle x, y\\rangle \\;:=\\; \\sum_{k<n} x_k\\,y_k ,$$\n\nthe finite sum of Finite sums and finite products, by recursion applied to the list $k \\mapsto x_k y_k$\n(extended by $0$ beyond $n$, as every finite list in this library is). The\n**Euclidean norm** of $x$ is\n\n$$\\lVert x\\rVert_2 \\;:=\\; \\sqrt{\\langle x, x\\rangle},$$\n\nwhich is defined because $\\langle x,x\\rangle = \\sum_{k<n} x_k^{2} \\ge 0$ (a sum\nof nonnegative terms, Laws of finite sums and finite products clause 4 and\nSquares of nonzero elements are positive, the case $x_k = 0$ giving $x_k^2 = 0$ by\nInteger powers $a^m$) and every nonnegative real has a unique nonnegative square\nroot (Square roots exist: a unique $\\sqrt{a} \\ge 0$ with $(\\sqrt{a})^2 = a$; the positives are $\\{x^2 : x \\neq 0\\}$).\n\n### Both are defined for every $n$, including $n = 0$\n\nAt $n = 0$ the set $\\mathbb{R}^{0}$ has exactly one element, the empty function,\nand it is the zero vector space (The standard list $e : n \\to F^{n}$ with $e_i(i) = 1_F$ and $e_i(j) = 0_F$ for $j \\ne i$ is an ordered basis of $F^{n}$; hence $\\dim_F F^{n} = n$, and $F^{0}$ is the zero space with basis $\\varnothing$ and dimension $0$ clause 5); the sum\nabove is the empty sum, so $\\langle x,y\\rangle = 0$ and $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = 0$.\n**This is the first place on this page where the two index regimes diverge, and\nthe divergence is deliberate.** The published metrics $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ of\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ as the set of functions $n \\to \\mathbb{R}$, and $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ are metrics on it are defined only for $n \\ge 1$, because $d_\\infty$ would\notherwise be a maximum over the empty index set; the algebra above needs no such\nrestriction. The boundary in this page runs between the algebra and the metric,\nnot where a reader would guess, and Conventions of this page, the standing $n \\ge 1$ hypothesis, and what is taken up elsewhere in the reading order lists\nexactly which items inherit $n \\ge 1$.\n\n### The algebra of the inner product\n\nFor all $x, y, z \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ and $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$:\n\n1. **Symmetry.** $\\langle x,y\\rangle = \\langle y,x\\rangle$, since $x_ky_k = y_kx_k$\n   termwise.\n2. **Additivity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle x + y, z\\rangle = \\langle x,z\\rangle + \\langle y,z\\rangle$: the list\n   $k \\mapsto (x_k+y_k)z_k$ is the termwise sum of $k \\mapsto x_kz_k$ and\n   $k \\mapsto y_kz_k$, so Laws of finite sums and finite products clause 1 applies.\n3. **Homogeneity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle \\lambda x, y\\rangle = \\lambda\\langle x,y\\rangle$, by\n   Laws of finite sums and finite products clause 2.\n4. **Bilinearity.** Clauses 2 and 3 together with symmetry give the same two laws\n   in the second argument.\n5. **Positive definiteness.** $\\langle x,x\\rangle \\ge 0$, and\n   $\\langle x,x\\rangle = 0$ if and only if $x = 0$. Indeed a vanishing sum of\n   nonnegative terms has every term $0$ (Laws of finite sums and finite products clause 4), so\n   $x_k^{2} = 0$ for every $k < n$, and a nonzero real has a positive square\n   (Squares of nonzero elements are positive), whence $x_k = 0$ for every $k < n$ and $x = 0$.\n6. **Agreement with the published Euclidean metric.** For $n \\ge 1$ and\n   $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$,\n   $\\lVert x - y\\rVert_2 = \\sqrt{\\sum_{k<n}(x_k-y_k)^{2}} = d_2(x,y)$, the two\n   sides being the same expression ($\\mathbb{R}^n$ as the set of functions $n \\to \\mathbb{R}$, and $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ are metrics on it). In particular\n   $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = d_2(x, 0)$.\n\nThat $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ is a norm in the sense of A norm on a real vector space, the induced metric, and the dictionary with the metric axioms\nis proved in Cauchy-Schwarz $\\lvert\\langle x,y\\rangle\\rvert \\le \\lVert x\\rVert_2\\lVert y\\rVert_2$ with its equality case, the triangle inequality for $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$, the parallelogram law and polarisation, where the triangle\ninequality is obtained from the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality; it is not assumed\nhere.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-projection-onto-a-nonempty-closed-convex-set",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $H$ be a real or complex Hilbert space, let $C\\subseteq H$ be nonempty, closed and convex, and let $x\\in H$. Then there is exactly one point $p\\in C$ with $\\|x-p\\|=\\inf_{c\\in C}\\|x-c\\|$, that is, a unique nearest point of $C$ to $x$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-lmnn-explicit-subgradient-and-data-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Fix the finite labeled sample, fixed target sets, and $\\mu\\in[0,1]$ of the LMNN objective. For each $j\\in N_i$ and each impostor index $l$ with $y_l\\ne y_i$, set $C_{ij}=(x_i-x_j)(x_i-x_j)^T$, $D_{ijl}=C_{ij}-C_{il}$, and $h_{ijl}(M)=1+\\langle M,D_{ijl}\\rangle_F$, where $\\langle A,B\\rangle_F=\\sum_{r,s}A_{rs}B_{rs}$. Define $\\gamma_{ijl}(M)=1$ if $h_{ijl}(M)>0$ and $\\gamma_{ijl}(M)=0$ if $h_{ijl}(M)\\le0$. Put\n\n$$G(M)=(1-\\mu)\\sum_i\\sum_{j\\in N_i}C_{ij}+\\mu\\sum_i\\sum_{j\\in N_i}\\sum_{l:y_l\\ne y_i}\\gamma_{ijl}(M)D_{ijl}.$$\n\nFor the convex objective $F_\\mu$ on $\\mathbb S_+^d$, a Frobenius subgradient at $M$ means a symmetric matrix $G$ such that $F_\\mu(N)\\ge F_\\mu(M)+\\langle G,N-M\\rangle_F$ for every $N\\in\\mathbb S_+^d$. Then $G(M)$ is a subgradient at every $M\\in\\mathbb S_+^d$. Moreover, uniformly in $M$,\n\n$$\\|G(M)\\|_F\\le B_{\\rm data}:=(1-\\mu)\\sum_i\\sum_{j\\in N_i}\\|x_i-x_j\\|_2^2+\\mu\\sum_i\\sum_{j\\in N_i}\\sum_{l:y_l\\ne y_i}\\bigl(\\|x_i-x_j\\|_2^2+\\|x_i-x_l\\|_2^2\\bigr).$$\n\nAt an exact hinge kink $h_{ijl}(M)=0$, the selected subgradient coefficient is $0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "alg-projected-subgradient-method",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Finite sums of vectors in $\\mathbb R^n$ use the additive finite-list sum of [[def-linear-combination-and-span]]; sums of real coefficients use [[def-finite-sum]].\n\nLet $n\\ge1$, let $C\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be nonempty, closed, and convex, and let $f:C\\to\\mathbb R$ be convex. The finite-budget projected subgradient procedure takes $x_0\\in C$, an integer budget $N\\ge1$, and positive stepsizes $\\alpha_0,\\ldots,\\alpha_{N-1}$. At iteration $k$, an oracle returns either failure or a pair $(f(x_k),g_k)$ with $g_k\\in\\partial f(x_k)$, where subgradient has the meaning in [[def-subgradient-and-subdifferential]]. On a valid response set\n$$x_{k+1}=P_C(x_k-\\alpha_k g_k),$$\nwhere $P_C(u)$ is the unique nearest point in $C$ to $u$. On an oracle failure at iteration $k$, return status **oracle-failure**, the feasible current iterate, the completed update count, and the least-index best among values observed so far if any (otherwise report none); a full-horizon average is not returned. If all $N$ calls succeed, return status **budget-exhausted**, the final iterate, the weighted average $\\bar x_N=(\\sum_{k<N}\\alpha_kx_k)/(\\sum_{k<N}\\alpha_k)$, and a least-index minimizer of observed values among $x_0,\\ldots,x_{N-1}$. Report status and completed update count. This finite procedure makes no convergence claim.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-projected-subgradient-best-and-average-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $C\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be nonempty, closed, and convex, let $f:C\\to\\mathbb R$ be convex, and run [[alg-projected-subgradient-method]] for $N\\ge1$ steps with positive $\\alpha_k$. Assume all $N$ oracle calls return valid pairs. Suppose a global minimizer $x^*\\in C$ exists and the oracle vectors obey $\\|g_k\\|_2\\le G$; let $R\\ge\\|x_0-x^*\\|_2$. Set $A_N=\\sum_{k<N}\\alpha_k>0$ and $\\bar x_N=A_N^{-1}\\sum_{k<N}\\alpha_kx_k$. Then\n$$\\max\\left\\{\\min_{0\\le k<N}\\bigl(f(x_k)-f(x^*)\\bigr),\\ f(\\bar x_N)-f(x^*)\\right\\}\\le\\frac{R^2+G^2\\sum_{k<N}\\alpha_k^2}{2A_N}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-frobenius-projection-onto-psd-cone-is-spectral-clipping",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$ and let $A\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$ be symmetric. There exists an orthogonal eigendecomposition $A=U\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_d)U^T$. For any such decomposition define $\\lambda_i^+=\\max\\{\\lambda_i,0\\}$ and $A_+=U\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1^+,\\ldots,\\lambda_d^+)U^T$. Let $\\mathbb S_+^d$ be the set of real symmetric matrices $Z$ with $v^TZv\\ge0$ for every $v\\in\\mathbb R^d$, and let $\\|B\\|_F=(\\sum_{i,j}B_{ij}^2)^{1/2}$. Then $A_+$ is the unique minimizer of $\\|A-Z\\|_F$ over $Z\\in\\mathbb S_+^d$, so its value is independent of the chosen orthogonal eigendecomposition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Put $m=d(d+1)/2$ and $C'=\\operatorname{svec}(\\mathbb S_+^d)$. The cone contains $0$; [F1] makes it convex, [F2] makes it closed in Frobenius distance, and [F3] transfers both properties to $C'$. By [F4], $\\mathbb R^m$ is a real Hilbert space; under [A1], [F5] gives a unique Euclidean projection onto $C'$, which is the projection used in [F7]. The explicit matrix update is that projection because [F9] identifies the unique Frobenius-nearest PSD matrix.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
