        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-mahalanobis-pseudometric",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix an integer $d\\ge1$. A real matrix $M\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$ is positive semidefinite, written $M\\succeq0$, when it is symmetric and $v^{T}Mv\\ge0$ for every $v\\in\\mathbb R^d$. For $x,x'\\in\\mathbb R^d$, define the squared Mahalanobis distance and its nonnegative square root by\n\n$$q_M(x,x') := (x-x')^{T}M(x-x'),\\qquad d_M(x,x') := \\sqrt{q_M(x,x')}.$$\n\nThe square root is defined because $q_M(x,x')\\ge0$. A pseudometric is a nonnegative symmetric function that vanishes on the diagonal and satisfies the triangle inequality, but may assign distance zero to distinct points. The function $d_M$ is called the Mahalanobis pseudometric associated with $M$; $q_M=d_M^2$ is its squared-distance form. The theorem on this page proves the pseudometric properties and the equivalent linear-feature representation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-mahalanobis-distance-is-pseudometric",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$ and let $M\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$ be real symmetric and positive semidefinite, meaning $v^TMv\\ge0$ for every $v\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Define $q_M(x,x')=(x-x')^TM(x-x')$ and $d_M(x,x')=\\sqrt{q_M(x,x')}$. Then there is a real matrix $L\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$ with $M=L^TL$ and, for all $x,x'\\in\\mathbb R^d$, $d_M(x,x')=\\|Lx-Lx'\\|_2$. Consequently $d_M$ is nonnegative, symmetric, zero on the diagonal, and satisfies the triangle inequality, without asserting separation of distinct points.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The matrix $M$ is symmetric, and for every $(a,b)\\in\\mathbb R^2$, $(a,b)M(a,b)^T=a^2\\ge0$; its rank is one, so it is singular. Also $u\\ne v$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "The difference $u-v=(0,-1)$ satisfies $(u-v)^TM(u-v)=0$, hence $q_M(u,v)=0$ and $d_M(u,v)=\\sqrt0=0$ by [F1].",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Step 1.2 exhibits zero distance between distinct points, so this valid Mahalanobis pseudometric fails the separation property required of a metric; [F2] confirms its other pseudometric axioms.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "F2"
          ]
        }
      ],
