    "ex-one-lmnn-projected-step-is-not-convergence": {
      "risk": "medium",
      "kind": "example",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-lmnn-fixed-target-objective",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix positive integers $d,n,C$, a finite labeled sample $(x_i,y_i)_{i=1}^{n}$ with $x_i\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and $y_i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,C\\}$, and a weight $\\mu\\in[0,1]$. For each $i$, fix in advance an integer $k_i$ with $0\\le k_i\\le|\\{j\\ne i:y_j=y_i\\}|$ and a fixed target-neighbor set $N_i\\subseteq\\{j\\ne i:y_j=y_i\\}$ with $|N_i|=k_i$. These target sets do not change with the matrix being optimized. Let $\\mathbb S_+^d$ denote the real symmetric matrices $M$ satisfying $v^TMv\\ge0$ for all $v\\in\\mathbb R^d$, and put $q_M(x,x')=(x-x')^TM(x-x')$. For a real number $z$, let $[z]_+=\\max\\{z,0\\}$. For $M\\in\\mathbb S_+^d$, define\n\n$$\\epsilon_{\\rm pull}(M)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\sum_{j\\in N_i}q_M(x_i,x_j),\\qquad \\epsilon_{\\rm push}(M)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\sum_{j\\in N_i}\\sum_{\\substack{1\\le l\\le n\\\\y_l\\ne y_i}}[1+q_M(x_i,x_j)-q_M(x_i,x_l)]_+,$$\n\nand $F_\\mu(M)=(1-\\mu)\\epsilon_{\\rm pull}(M)+\\mu\\epsilon_{\\rm push}(M)$. This is the fixed-target LMNN training surrogate; it is not a test-error or calibrated/generalization-risk quantity. The quantity $q_M$ is a squared distance, while its square root is the Mahalanobis pseudometric.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-lmnn-explicit-subgradient-and-data-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Fix the finite labeled sample, fixed target sets, and $\\mu\\in[0,1]$ of the LMNN objective. For each $j\\in N_i$ and each impostor index $l$ with $y_l\\ne y_i$, set $C_{ij}=(x_i-x_j)(x_i-x_j)^T$, $D_{ijl}=C_{ij}-C_{il}$, and $h_{ijl}(M)=1+\\langle M,D_{ijl}\\rangle_F$, where $\\langle A,B\\rangle_F=\\sum_{r,s}A_{rs}B_{rs}$. Define $\\gamma_{ijl}(M)=1$ if $h_{ijl}(M)>0$ and $\\gamma_{ijl}(M)=0$ if $h_{ijl}(M)\\le0$. Put\n\n$$G(M)=(1-\\mu)\\sum_i\\sum_{j\\in N_i}C_{ij}+\\mu\\sum_i\\sum_{j\\in N_i}\\sum_{l:y_l\\ne y_i}\\gamma_{ijl}(M)D_{ijl}.$$\n\nFor the convex objective $F_\\mu$ on $\\mathbb S_+^d$, a Frobenius subgradient at $M$ means a symmetric matrix $G$ such that $F_\\mu(N)\\ge F_\\mu(M)+\\langle G,N-M\\rangle_F$ for every $N\\in\\mathbb S_+^d$. Then $G(M)$ is a subgradient at every $M\\in\\mathbb S_+^d$. Moreover, uniformly in $M$,\n\n$$\\|G(M)\\|_F\\le B_{\\rm data}:=(1-\\mu)\\sum_i\\sum_{j\\in N_i}\\|x_i-x_j\\|_2^2+\\mu\\sum_i\\sum_{j\\in N_i}\\sum_{l:y_l\\ne y_i}\\bigl(\\|x_i-x_j\\|_2^2+\\|x_i-x_l\\|_2^2\\bigr).$$\n\nAt an exact hinge kink $h_{ijl}(M)=0$, the selected subgradient coefficient is $0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "alg-lmnn-finite-budget-projected-subgradient",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the finite sample, fixed target sets, weight $\\mu\\in[0,1]$, and objective $F_\\mu$ defined by the LMNN objective item. The inputs are an integer budget $T\\ge1$, an initial matrix $M_0\\in\\mathbb S_+^d$, and positive step sizes $\\eta_0,\\ldots,\\eta_{T-1}$. Let $m=d(d+1)/2$ and use $\\operatorname{svec}:\\mathbb S^d\\to\\mathbb R^m$, the linear isometry that lists the diagonal entries followed by the $\\sqrt2$-scaled upper-triangle entries in lexicographic order. Set $C'=\\operatorname{svec}(\\mathbb S_+^d)$ and $f(z)=F_\\mu(\\operatorname{svec}^{-1}(z))$; this is a nonempty closed convex domain and $f$ is convex.\n\nInitialize $z_0=\\operatorname{svec}(M_0)$. At each $t=0,\\ldots,T-1$, put $M_t=\\operatorname{svec}^{-1}(z_t)$ and compute the Frobenius subgradient\n\n$$G_t=(1-\\mu)\\sum_i\\sum_{j\\in N_i}C_{ij}+\\mu\\sum_i\\sum_{j\\in N_i}\\sum_{l:y_l\\ne y_i}\\gamma_{ijl}(M_t)(C_{ij}-C_{il}),$$\n\nwhere $C_{ij}=(x_i-x_j)(x_i-x_j)^T$, $h_{ijl}(M)=1+\\langle M,C_{ij}-C_{il}\\rangle_F$, $\\gamma_{ijl}(M)=1$ when $h_{ijl}(M)>0$, and $\\gamma_{ijl}(M)=0$ when $h_{ijl}(M)\\le0$. Supply the successful oracle response $(f(z_t),\\operatorname{svec}(G_t))$ to the finite-budget projected-subgradient method on $C'$ with step size $\\eta_t$. It updates $z_{t+1}=P_{C'}(z_t-\\eta_t\\operatorname{svec}(G_t))$, where $P_{C'}$ is the unique nearest point in the Euclidean norm. Equivalently, form $A_t=M_t-\\eta_tG_t$, choose an orthogonal eigendecomposition $A_t=U_t\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_{t,1},\\ldots,\\lambda_{t,d})U_t^T$, and set\n\n$$M_{t+1}=U_t\\operatorname{diag}\\bigl(\\max\\{\\lambda_{t,1},0\\},\\ldots,\\max\\{\\lambda_{t,d},0\\}\\bigr)U_t^T.$$\n\nAll $T$ oracle calls succeed; the generic method reports budget-exhausted after $T$ updates. The requested final iterate is $M_T$; its status, completed update count, weighted average, and least-index best observed iterate are also defined by the generic method. The explicit spectral-clipping implementation is choice-free. The equivalent abstract Hilbert-projection interface assumes Countable Choice, as in the general closest-point theorem; that axiom branch is retained separately for the conditional analysis. This finite procedure makes no unconditional convergence, optimality, empirical-error, or generalization claim.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For a scalar feasible matrix $M=[m]$, $q_M(0,1)=q_M(1,0)=m$. Since the labels agree, there are no impostor terms; hence $F_0([m])=2m$, which is minimized over $m\\ge0$ at $m=0$. Therefore $F_0(M_0)=4$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "The two pull matrices are both $[1]$, and $\\mu=0$, so the selected subgradient is $G_0=[2]$ by [F2]. The update [F3] clips $M_0-\\eta_0G_0=[2-\\tfrac14\\cdot2]=[\\tfrac32]$ to the PSD cone, leaving $M_1=[\\tfrac32]$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
